/**
 * One-off backfill for `Server.termPromoPercent` (added 2026-07-26).
 *
 * WHY. `prorateDeltaCents` used to default its rate basis to `currentPromo(now)`,
 * so a resize was priced off whatever seasonal promo happened to be running when
 * the customer clicked — letting them upgrade under a 45% basis and downgrade under
 * a 15% one and keep the spread on every remaining month. The fix records the rate
 * each term was ACTUALLY sold at on the server row. Rows that predate the column
 * are NULL, and NULL must never quietly fall back to today's promo, because that is
 * the bug itself. This script fills them from the billing record.
 *
 * HOW, in strict order of evidence. Each row reports which rule fired.
 *
 *   1. free / no expiry      -> 100 (gifted; nothing was sold, nothing prorates)
 *   2. game plan             -> 0   (orderGameServer charges gp.priceCents flat:
 *                                    no term discount, no promo, and renewalCharge
 *                                    renews at that same flat price)
 *   3. INVOICE for the current term -> the percent stated on the document
 *   4. WALLET TRANSACTION for the order -> same, for rows that predate invoicing
 *   5. otherwise             -> the conservative fallback (see below)
 *
 * Rules 3 and 4 read what the customer was genuinely billed. Both an order and a
 * renewal write their description from the same templates the code still uses:
 *   order:   "Order: <Plan> VPS (<hostname>) — <Term> · <Promo> NN% off first term"
 *   renewal: "Renewal: <Plan> VPS (<hostname>) — <Term>"          (no promo, ever)
 * so "NN% off first term" is the sold percent, and a renewal is 0% by definition —
 * lib/promotions.ts guarantees renewals never receive the intro discount. The
 * current term's document is identified by `periodEnd` landing on the server's
 * `expiresAt` (they are written from two separate `new Date()` calls milliseconds
 * apart, hence the tolerance) AND by carrying the hostname, so one user's several
 * servers cannot cross-match.
 *
 * Every derived percent is CROSS-CHECKED arithmetically against the cash actually
 * taken: recompute what that percent implies the charge should have been and
 * compare. A mismatch is reported loudly rather than written silently.
 *
 * THE FALLBACK IS NOT "FULL PRICE". The intuitive conservative guess — assume they
 * paid undiscounted list — is the money-printing direction: a credit is
 * monotonically increasing in the rate basis, so 0% off produces the LARGEST credit
 * this platform can emit, and for anyone who really bought under a deep promo it
 * pays out more cash than they ever handed over. The safe assumption is the
 * opposite: the LEAST we have ever charged (`deepestPromoPercent()`). See
 * lib/proration.ts `resolveTermPromoPercent` for the worked numbers.
 *
 * IDEMPOTENT: only ever touches rows where the column IS NULL. Re-running is a
 * no-op. Pass --apply to write; the default is a dry run.
 *
 *   npx tsx scripts/backfill-term-promo-percent.ts            # dry run
 *   npx tsx scripts/backfill-term-promo-percent.ts --apply    # write
 */

